-- chem/selection.cub — the selection theorem, stated, and its smallest instance computed (docs/chemical-identity.md,
-- "Notes, uncertainty first" (c), section X). The statement, for a finite space of sequences s (residues over an
-- alphabet), a finite space of folds c (contact maps: conformations with equal contact data identified, the fold as a
-- fiber) counted by n_c conformations, and a contact energy E_s(c):
--   the native fold c*(s) is the unique fold of least energy, when it is unique; the misfolds are the others, weighted
--   by their conformations; δE(s) = mean misfold energy − E_s(c*) and ΔE(s)² = the misfold energies' variance (the
--   random energy model's gap and width); z(s) = δE²/(δE² + ΔE²) ∈ (0, 1], increasing in δE/ΔE, and z = 0 when the
--   least energy is not unique. Selection is the Metropolis chain on sequences: a uniformly chosen residue changed,
--   accepted with probability min(1, w(z(s′))/w(z(s))), w positive and increasing. Claim: (ix) the chain's stationary
--   distribution is w(z(s))/Σ w (detailed balance; the mutation graph is connected); (ii) it puts on the sequences
--   with z ≥ θ at least their share under neutral drift (w increasing, the set an upper set of z); (iii) the
--   information the sequence carries about its fold, read as the Boltzmann probability p(s) of its native fold at
--   fugacity x > 1 per favourable contact, is bounded below on z ≥ θ > 0 by g = x/(x + T), T the number of
--   conformations: a unique least energy puts every misfold at least one contact above it.
-- Frustration, as Toulouse's cycles of contact constraints that cannot all hold, is the homology of the contact-
-- constraint complex: a definition stated here, not computed.
-- The instance: the HP model (Lau and Dill 1989) on the square lattice, 6 residues, H = 1 and P = 0 bits of s ∈ [0, 64),
-- E_s(c) = −(the number of H–H contacts in c); the contacts possible are (0,3), (0,5), (1,4), (2,5), a fold is a 4-bit
-- code, and every walk of 5 steps with the first step fixed is enumerated (`sl-hist`). Checked: detailed balance at every
-- pair of neighbouring sequences, the enrichment at θ = 1/2 (δE ≥ ΔE) and the information bound at x = 2, with
-- w(z) = 1 + 8z (`sel-instance`); the instance's z and p are tabulated once and passed.
-- A theorem: (ix) and (ii) for every finite instance (detailed balance of Metropolis; Chebyshev's sum inequality),
-- (iii) for integer contact energies. Checked by computation: all three in the instance. A definition: z, w, p and the
-- reading of information as p. Open: the bound with δE/ΔE entering beyond its sign (information growing with the gap),
-- frustration's homology computed, and the instance on the interaction complex in place of a lattice.
